<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llangollen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Llangollen is a small town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbighshire">Denbighshire</a>, north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, on the River Dee. It is named after the 6th-century saint Collen. Nearby stand the ruined Cistercian abbey of Valle Crucis, founded in 1201 by Madog ap Gruffydd of Powys Fadog, and the 9th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pillar-of-eliseg">Pillar of Eliseg</a>. Thomas Telford's Pontcysyllte <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">Aqueduct</a> lies close by, and the town has hosted the International Musical Eisteddfod since 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/llangollen.jpg" alt="Llangollen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Warren 1973 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Llangollen_Church.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Llangollen is a town and community on the River Dee in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbighshire">Denbighshire</a>. Its riverside site forms the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwyn-range">Berwyn range</a> and lies within the Dee Valley section of the Clwydian Range and Dee Valley Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty.</p><p>The name combines the Welsh llan, a religious settlement, with Saint Collen, a monk who founded a church beside the river and is said to have arrived by coracle. St Collen's Church is the only church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> dedicated to him. Above the town to the north stands <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castell-dinas-bran">Castell Dinas Brân</a>, a stronghold of the Princes of Powys, beyond which runs the limestone escarpment of the Eglwyseg Rocks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valle-crucis-abbey">Valle Crucis Abbey</a> was established at Llantysilio in about 1201 under the patronage of Madog ap Gruffydd Maelor. The bridge over the Dee was built in the sixteenth century to replace one put up about 1345 by John Trevor, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of St Asaph, which had replaced an even earlier bridge of the reign of Henry I. An anonymous rhyme counts it among the Seven Wonders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. From 1780 Plas Newydd was the home of the Ladies of Llangollen, Sarah Ponsonby and Lady Eleanor Butler, and their maid Mary Carryl, who share a grave memorial in the church.</p><p>Llangollen was an ancient parish in the commote of Nanheudwy, which became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbighshire">Denbighshire</a> in 1536. A local board was set up in 1857, and the town hall was built in 1867. The railway from Ruabon reached the town by 1865 and closed in 1965, though ten miles of it between Llangollen and Corwen have been restored as a tourist line. A feeder canal from the Ellesmere Canal, taking water from the Dee at Horseshoe Falls, also reaches the town. The hills around the town were mostly sheep farms, and wool and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> were processed in factories along the Dee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The community had 3,603 inhabitants at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and the town relies heavily on tourism. It hosted the National Eisteddfod in 1908, and each year it holds the Llangollen International Eisteddfod, a large international music festival that opens with a parade led by the Llangollen Silver Band. The Dee hosts international white-water canoe slalom events. The nursery rhyme Mary had a little lamb is often but wrongly linked with the town; its true origin is in Massachusetts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Llangollen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…that later ruled the kingdom traced its descent from Cadell Ddyrnllug, and the ninth-century Pillar of Eliseg near <b>Llangollen</b> linked the royal line to Vortigern and to Magnus Maximus, the Roman usurper of 383–388; these claims show how later kings wished to be seen rather than reliable history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its fame grew after the <b>Llangollen</b> Eisteddfod of 1858, where Thomas Llewelyn of Aberdare won a competition for an unpublished collection of Welsh airs with a collection that included &quot;Glan Rhondda&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hen-wlad-fy-nhadau">Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</a></li></ul></section>
